{{Japanese name|Ohno}}

'''Yoshimizu Ohno''' (大野 吉水, ''Ōno Yoshimizu''; 12 April 1908 – 23 November 1991) was a Sanese politician who served as the Prime Minister of Okaiken on two separate occasions: first as the 44th Prime Minister under the Empire of Mitoyagi (1964–1966), and later as the first Prime Minister of the Federal Republic of Okaiken (1968–1970).

A pivotal figure in modern Sanese history, Ohno is widely regarded as the "Father of the Republic." He led the democratic opposition to the authoritarian government of [[Genzui Kusaka]], uniting the fractured political center into the [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]] during the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968.

His second administration oversaw the '''Great Transformation''' ({{nihongo|大転換|Dai-Tenkan}}), a series of radical reforms that dismantled the Imperial system, drafted the 1970 [[Constitution of Okaiken]], and established the modern secular state.

==Early life and career==
Ohno was born in [[Sendai]], [[Miyagi Prefecture]], into a family of wealthy silk merchants. He studied economics at the [[Imperial University of Okami]], graduating in 1932. Unlike many of his contemporaries who joined the military or the bureaucracy, Ohno entered the private sector, working for the [[Sanese Central Bank]].

During the [[Great Adonian War]], he served as a civilian economic advisor, gaining a reputation for his quiet competence and his private skepticism of the military government. After the war, he ran for the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet]] in the first post-war election of 1946, winning a seat as a member of the moderate Liberal faction.

==First Premiership (1964–1966)==
By 1964, Ohno had risen to become the leader of the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO). He became Prime Minister in October 1964 after the collapse of [[Tadashige Honda]]'s Progressive government.

His first term was plagued by the "Stagnation of the Sixties." While a capable administrator, Ohno struggled to manage the growing labor unrest and the radicalization of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP). His moderate economic policies failed to curb inflation, and his administration was severely damaged by allegations of voter fraud in rural districts. In the [[Okaiken general election, 1966|1966 general election]], he was defeated by the OSP's charismatic firebrand, [[Genzui Kusaka]].

==The Quiet Revolution (1968)==
Ohno spent the next two years as the Leader of the Opposition. As Prime Minister Kusaka moved to dismantle the Diet and establish a "Statist" dictatorship, Ohno worked tirelessly to unite the fractured opposition parties (the DPO, the Progressives, and disaffected liberals) into a single "National Salvation Front," which would later become the [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]].

When Kusaka attempted to dissolve parliament in July 1968, triggering mass protests, Ohno became the political face of the [[Quiet Revolution]]. He famously refused to call for violence, instead organizing a "Legislative Strike" where opposition members refused to leave the Diet building.

On August 12, 1968, following Kusaka's removal by security forces, Ohno was invited by [[Emperor Wakayama]] to form a provisional government of national unity.

==Second Premiership (1968–1970)==
Ohno's second term was the most transformative in the nation's history. He was granted extraordinary powers to restructure the state.

===The Great Transformation===
Ohno argued that the Imperial system had been the root cause of both the fascist military takeover in the 1930s and Kusaka's socialist authoritarianism in the 1960s. He proposed the "Great Transformation," a complete separation of the Emperor from the state.

* '''Asset Seizure:''' His government passed the Imperial Assets Act, seizing the vast landholdings and financial assets of the Imperial Household to pay off national debts and fund reconstruction.
* '''The 1970 Constitution:''' Ohno oversaw the drafting of the new constitution, which redefined the Emperor as a "symbol of the state" with no political power and enshrined pacifism and human rights as the core values of the nation.

===Resignation===
The reforms were successful but deeply polarizing. Ohno faced immense backlash from traditionalists and monarchists for his "disrespect" toward the Imperial Family. Feeling that his work was done and that the new Republic needed a fresh leader to heal the divide, Ohno resigned on July 19, 1970, handing power to his younger protégé, [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]].

==Later life==
Ohno retired from the Diet in 1979. He spent his final years as an elder statesman, advising subsequent Prime Ministers on constitutional matters. He died in 1991 at the age of 83.

